      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}$ when $f(x)=(8+6x)^{6}.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf=g^{6},\\,g=8+6x\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}=6g^{5},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}=6.\n$$\n\nFinally, multiplying the two together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} &=6\\cdot 6\\cdot (8+6x)^{5} \\\\  &=36(8+6x)^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
